=====================================================================
lisptc — working with JSON data

There is no JSON type. JSON is converted at the edge — an MCP tool
result arrives already as lisptc data, whether the server sent
structured output or a JSON document in a text block — so "working
with JSON" is just working with alists and lists:

  JSON object    ->  alist: a list of pairs with STRING keys
  JSON array     ->  proper list
  string, number ->  string, number
  true           ->  t
  false, null    ->  nil

The reverse conversion happens when a value is serialized into an
outgoing MCP call: an alist becomes an object, any other list becomes
an array. See section 9. A string of JSON that reaches you some other
way is what json-parse is for; there is no json-stringify.

Run me:
  pnpm repl examples/json.ptc
=====================================================================
(echo "=== 1. what a JSON document looks like as data ===")

A quoted literal is the clearest way to see the shape. This is the
same structure a tool call would hand you.
(setq issue '(("id" . "a1f")
    ("identifier" . "ENG-12")
    ("title" . "Auth token refresh fails")
    ("open" . t)
    ("state" . (("name" . "open") ("type" . "unstarted")))
    ("labels" . ("auth" "backend"))
    ("assignee" . (("name" . "Ada") ("email" . "ada@x.dev")))))
The REPL reports its shape, not its contents. Echo it to see it.
(echo issue)

(echo "=== 2. reading one field ===")

assoc finds the pair; cdr takes the value out of it.
(echo (assoc "title" issue))
=> the whole pair, key included
(echo (cdr (assoc "title" issue)))
=> Auth token refresh fails
A missing key is nil, and cdr of nil is nil — so a lookup that fails
is nil rather than an error. Beware: a key whose value is JSON null
or false is ALSO nil, so nil does not tell you which happened.
(echo (cdr (assoc "nope" issue)))
=> nil
assq is the same thing by eq instead of equal — use it for symbol or
keyword keys, not for the string keys JSON gives you.

(echo "=== 3. keys and values of an object ===")

There is no keys built-in: an object is a list of pairs, so the keys
are its cars and the values are its cdrs.
(defun keys (obj) (mapcar car obj))
(defun vals (obj) (mapcar cdr obj))
(echo (keys issue))
=> id identifier title open state labels assignee
(echo (keys (cdr (assoc "assignee" issue))))
=> name email
(echo (vals (cdr (assoc "state" issue))))
=> open unstarted
Key order is the order the JSON had — the conversion preserves it.
(echo (length (keys issue)))
=> 7

(echo "=== 4. sub-keys: reaching into nested objects ===")

Nesting is just an alist whose value is another alist, so the lookups
chain.
(echo (cdr (assoc "name" (cdr (assoc "state" issue)))))
=> open
That gets unreadable fast, so define a path getter once. There is no
deep-get in the prelude.
(defun get-in (obj path)
  "Follow a list of string keys into nested objects; nil if absent."
  (if (null path)
    obj
    (get-in (cdr (assoc (car path) obj)) (cdr path))))
(echo (get-in issue '("state" "name")))
=> open
(echo (get-in issue '("assignee" "email")))
=> ada@x.dev
A path that runs off the document is nil, not an error.
(echo (get-in issue '("assignee" "manager" "email")))
=> nil

(echo "=== 5. every key at every depth ===")

Walk the document: at an object, take its keys and recurse into each
value; at an array, recurse into each element.
(defun alistp (x)
  "Return t if x is a non-empty list of pairs with string keys."
  (and (consp x)
    (let ((ok t))
      (dolist (e x)
        (unless (and (consp e) (stringp (car e))) (setq ok nil)))
      ok)))

(defun deep-keys (x)
  "Every key in x and in everything nested inside it, in order."
  (let ((out nil))
    (cond ((alistp x)
        (dolist (pair x)
          (setq out (append out
              (list (car pair))
              (deep-keys (cdr pair))))))
      ((consp x)
        (dolist (item x) (setq out (append out (deep-keys item))))))
    out))
(echo (deep-keys issue))
=> the outer keys plus name/type from state and name/email from assignee
Repeats are kept, since a key can appear at several depths. Dedupe
with member if you want the set.
(defun distinct (xs)
  "Drop later duplicates, keeping first appearance order."
  (let ((out nil))
    (dolist (x xs)
      (unless (member x out) (setq out (append out (list x)))))
    out))
(echo (distinct (deep-keys issue)))

(echo "=== 6. looping over an array of objects ===")

An array of objects is a list of alists — the shape a list-style tool
result usually has.
(setq issues (list issue
    '(("id" . "b2e")
      ("identifier" . "ENG-31")
      ("title" . "OAuth callback drops the state param")
      ("open" . t)
      ("state" . (("name" . "in progress") ("type" . "started")))
      ("labels" . ("auth" "frontend"))
      ("assignee" . (("name" . "Grace") ("email" . "grace@x.dev"))))
    '(("id" . "c3d")
      ("identifier" . "ENG-47")
      ("title" . "Rotate the signing key")
      ("open" . nil)
      ("state" . (("name" . "done") ("type" . "completed")))
      ("labels" . ("security"))
      ("assignee" . nil))))
You never have to print it to work with it: length and every accessor
below read the whole value whether or not you have seen any of it.
(echo (length issues))
=> 3

mapcar to pull one field out of every element.
(echo (mapcar (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "identifier" i))) issues))
=> ENG-12 ENG-31 ENG-47
Or a path, for a field one level down. A missing assignee is nil.
(echo (mapcar (lambda (i) (get-in i '("assignee" "name"))) issues))
=> Ada Grace nil

dolist when you want a side effect per element rather than a new list.
(dolist (i issues)
  (echo (concat (cdr (assoc "identifier" i))
      " -> "
      (get-in i '("state" "name")))))

Render a table for the user: one function for a row, string-join for
the whole thing, one echo at the end.
(defun row (i)
  (concat "| " (cdr (assoc "identifier" i))
    " | " (cdr (assoc "title" i))
    " | " (get-in i '("state" "name")) " |"))
(echo (string-join (cons "| id | title | state |"
      (cons "|----|-------|-------|"
        (mapcar row issues)))
    "\n"))

(echo "=== 7. selecting a subset ===")

grep RETURNS the matching elements of a list, so it is the filter you
already have. It matches against each element's printed form.
(echo (mapcar (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "identifier" i)))
    (grep issues "auth")))
=> ENG-12 ENG-31, the two whose text mentions auth
head and tail take elements, so they page an array.
(echo (mapcar (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "identifier" i))) (head issues 2)))
=> ENG-12 ENG-31

For a predicate on a field rather than on text, write the filter —
there is no filter or reduce in the prelude.
(defun where (pred xs)
  "The elements of xs for which pred returns non-nil."
  (let ((out nil))
    (dolist (x xs)
      (when (pred x) (setq out (append out (list x)))))
    out))
(echo (mapcar (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "identifier" i)))
    (where (lambda (i) (equal "done" (get-in i '("state" "name"))))
      issues)))
=> ENG-47
Counting is the same shape with a number instead of a list.
(defun count-where (pred xs)
  (let ((n 0))
    (dolist (x xs) (when (pred x) (setq n (+ n 1))))
    n))
(echo (count-where (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "open" i))) issues))
=> 2

Arrays inside an object are ordinary lists, so the same tools work on
them. Collect every label used, without repeats.
(echo (distinct (apply append (mapcar (lambda (i) (cdr (assoc "labels" i)))
        issues))))
=> auth backend frontend security

(echo "=== 8. reading a document too big to print ===")

Everything above fits on screen. A real tool result usually does not,
and echoing it wastes context. Prefer computing over reading:
  length, keys, mapcar, assoc and get-in all work on the whole value
  whether or not you have ever seen it.
When you do need to look, narrow first. :match prints only the regions
that match, each tagged with its word offset.
(echo issues :match "state" :context 4 :max 3)
And :offset with :length pages through, continuing from an offset the
REPL just gave you.
(echo issues :offset 0 :length 12)

(echo "=== 9. building JSON to send back out ===")

Nothing to serialize by hand: build the alist and pass it to a tool
call. An alist becomes a JSON object, any other list becomes an array,
t becomes true, nil becomes null.
(setq payload (list (cons "title" "Rotate the signing key")
    (cons "labels" (list "security" "auth"))
    (cons "priority" 2)
    (cons "assignee" (list (cons "email" "ada@x.dev")))))
(echo payload)
That value, handed to a tool as a keyword argument, arrives at the
other end as a JSON object with a nested object and an array inside
it. Keyword arguments at the call site are converted the same way, so
a call written with :title and :labels needs no JSON step of its own.

One asymmetry to remember: nil on the way out is null, and both false
and null on the way in are nil. A round trip turns false into null.
(echo (cdr (assoc "assignee" (car (cdr (cdr issues))))))
=> nil, which was JSON null here — the same value a false would give

(echo "=== 10. a JSON string is not JSON data ===")

If a tool hands you a STRING that happens to contain JSON, none of
the above applies: it is text until something parses it, and there is
no parser. Pull what you need out with grep, which returns the
matches, or with the string library — substring, string-index,
string-split, string-join.
(setq blob "{\"id\":\"a1f\",\"title\":\"Auth token refresh fails\"}")
(echo (grep blob "\"([a-z]+)\":" :group 1))
=> id title, the keys, as a list of strings
(echo (car (grep blob "\"title\":\"([^\"]*)\"" :group 1)))
=> Auth token refresh fails

Every helper this file defined — keys, vals, get-in, alistp,
deep-keys, distinct, where, count-where — is an ordinary defun, not a
built-in. The prelude ships none of them, so copy the ones you want
into your own session and build on them.

(echo "=== done ===")
